the first 5g pilots are launching in the  UK promising everything from smart  cities to hologram calls yet the rush to  build the superfast wireless network  comes with a risk because the best  technology comes from Chinese  manufacturers such as hawawa and ZTE  raising the fear that China's government  will gain ground level access to even  control of the UK's critical data  infrastructure prompting a former  security minister to urge the government  to act we've really got to grasp this  we've got to see there's a risk yes we  want 5g but goodness sake we need to do  all of these things to make sure it's  not putting us all at risk what I would  do is set up a small unit in the Cabinet  Office who'd be responsible to the  National Security Advisor and then on  through to the prime minister for coming  up with proposals that would ameliorate  the risk of using this Chinese equipment  a call which comes against their  backdrop of rising tensions in April  this year GCHQ x' national cybersecurity  center warned ZTE could pose a national  security risk in july the uk's hawawa  cybersecurity evaluation center a group  set up by the government to monitor the  chinese firm announced that it had only  limited assurance that hawaii posed no  threat to national security  finally in August the Australian  Government took the step of banning  hawawa and said te from supplying  technology for its 5g network in a  statement hawawa  expressed frustration at the move  calling the decision politically  motivated not the result of a fact-based  transparent or equitable decision-making  process there is no fundamental  difference between 5g and 4G network  architecture but the former director of  GCHQ says that safety and security are  based on trust which at the moment is in  short supply we do need to find a way of  scrutinizing what is being installed in  our networks and how it is  ever seen and how it's been controlled  and how it's being upgraded in the  future because very often this is about  the next generation and we have to find  a more effective way of doing that at  scale in the West VT which continues to  use huawei products told Sky News that  it will apply stringent security  measures and controls to 5g leaving the  next generation of mobile communication  vulnerable from it's very inception role  amantha Sky News
 

